2026-08-12 — implementation record

The RFC and implementation plan are immutable normative inputs. The cutover manifest is the
literal deletion inventory; this file records implementation progress and unresolved risks without
duplicating deleted protocol names.

The preflight commit recorded the two supported entry-point capability inventory, immutable
binding inventory, context ownership table, outer capacity envelope, durability profile, scope/
epoch rotation rule, and the typed ABI spike. The first implementation commit then established
the target package graph, singular fixed-tenant facade, State/Match-only Program shape, three-family
journal, Store-owned reduction, Runtime-owned affine execution owners, strict replay, EVM/Portfolio
value cutover, PostgreSQL mechanical boundary, and thread-affine keystore.

Current implementation status:

- Program, Capabilities, Journal, Store, Runtime, Replay, App, domains, adapters, and storage all
  compile in the default Nix shell.
- Store now validates exact selected preparations, replacement ordinals, direct State/Match
  continuity, fact request/selection parity, atomic conclusion candidates, object reachability,
  recursive heads, and bounded append capacity.
- Runtime now exposes call-correlated outcome, integrity-blocked, and unresolved access owners;
  only a direct-new preparation can produce a committed call. Assembly registration retains typed
  Pure/Access implementations and exact binding identities before a facade is created.
- EVM ingress converts malformed, unauthenticated, and ambiguous provider outcomes into the
  unresolved owner and bounds derived request/evidence values. Portfolio and EVM cumulative
  continuations validate declaration order, remaining work, asset Match payloads, and public
  integer-scaled results.
- Facts now carry an optional producer frontier and require selected completeness to attest the
  captured frontier. Configuration revisions have strict content identities, bounded affine
  writers, duplicate-request promotion, retained-prefix loading, and Memory/PostgreSQL parity
  surfaces.
- PostgreSQL loads verify run heads, frame digests, dense fact publication coordinates, selected
  publication references, and complete configuration prefixes before Store qualification.
- The manifest scanner is part of the Nix task graph and is required by the composite gate.

Latest audit hardening:

- The Store Match regression now proves that a selected payload can be the complete State input
  while the conclusion returns a distinct output; conclusion closure contains only reachable
  objects, while preparation closure retains the exact recorded input object.
- Runtime contains Pure evaluation, Access preparation, adapter construction, and future-poll
  panics as redacted neutral errors. Pending conclusion constructors are Runtime-private, and
  direct-new call correlation is generated only after the Store-owned preparation identity exists.
- EVM and Portfolio admission, cumulative-context, and public-result decoders re-enter bounded
  domain validation. EVM provider responses require the exact echoed call correlation and remain
  within the bounded response envelope.
- PostgreSQL duplicate-frame resolution verifies the recorded digest, admission writes enforce the
  opened tenant partition, and fact/configuration retained identities are checked before promotion.
- Focused workspace tests, the checked source scan, `git diff --check`, and the Nixfied model check
  pass on this tree.

Final delta and verification record:

- Public API now has one fixed-tenant `Application` facade, State/Match-only Program and
  three-family Journal values, Store-owned reduction and append qualification, Runtime-owned
  affine execution owners, typed access resolution, strict v5 replay, bounded EVM/Portfolio
  domain values, and mechanical PostgreSQL persistence. Removed public compatibility surfaces
  include the former structured operation/runtime/history/certification/spec APIs, generic append
  paths, provider-process authority surface, and the retired Bitcoin entry point.
- The workspace is reduced to 21 members and the lockfile to 218 package records. The direct
  dependency direction now runs from the fixed facade to domain/kernel contracts, from adapters
  to Runtime, and from storage to Journal/Store; retired blockchain, authority, certification,
  and legacy integration dependencies are absent.
- The cutover delta is 471 changed paths, 14,653 insertions, and 166,692 deletions; the current
  `bin`/`crates` tree contains 21,203 Rust lines, including the replacement contracts and tests.
  Persisted identity is now the tenant-scoped EVM idempotency key plus nonce domain (or the
  canonical Portfolio admission digest), with v5 portable-run content identity and PostgreSQL
  schema v8 single-trust records.
- `nix develop -c cargo fmt --all`, workspace check, all-target tests, all-features Clippy,
  `bash scripts/check-cutover-manifest.sh`, `nix run .#run -- --task negative-scan`, and
  `nix run .#model-check` pass. The final `nix run .#ci` pass reports 6 tasks passed and 0
  failed, including doc tests.
- The RFC, implementation plan, and cutover manifest remain byte-for-byte unchanged after the
  initial preflight commits. The source scan, secret-surface scan, dependency-direction audit,
  and `git diff --check` remain clean.
- Ordered commits are `94fa8a48` (`settle single trust boundary preflight rulings`), `b223ae17`
  (`resolve prepared-call execution gates`), `2cd39f94` (`cut over sequential trust-boundary
  runtime`), followed by this final verification record.

The implementation is complete; the remaining deliberate limits are primary crash/restart
durability, trusted in-process State and adapter code, process-loss response neutrality before
conclusion durability, and the two fixed entry points. The fixed-tenant App facade exposes
callback-free structural drive/read/replay and leaves live State/adapter registration to the
trusted Runtime embedding; it never exposes a successful output before a matching durable
conclusion. No external credential or tenant override is admitted by the public facades.

2026-08-12 — architect audit correction (no code implementation)

The prior completion statement is not supported by the committed tree. The following required
surfaces remain unimplemented and block completion:

- `mfm-runtime` has typed low-level owners and `RunSession<T>`, but no public `Runtime`,
  `AdmissionInput`, `ResumeInput`, `RuntimeStep`, exhaustive admission/resume lifecycle, or
  owner-fate coordinator. The fixed-tenant Application therefore performs callback-free reduction
  only and cannot drive a live State through the required lifecycle.
- `mfm-store` still exposes the memory-only `RunStore` semantic/mechanical object. There is no
  branded `StructuredStore::open`, split history/configuration/audit ports, `ActiveQualifiedRun`,
  or composite backend SPI. PostgreSQL independently duplicates semantic append/load behavior
  instead of implementing the Store mechanical boundary.
- The repository has no checked-in U3/U5 capability-binding inventory, U8/U11 typed ABI proof,
  or U1/U7/U9 capacity/reservation artifact. The log's prose claim is not an artifact or a
  machine-checked proof.
- There is no external API/trybuild exclusion suite, common Memory/PostgreSQL backend conformance
  harness, test-only owner/callback counters, finite state-model harness, or managed PostgreSQL
  crash/CAS verification task. The existing `test-support`, `backend-conformance`, and
  `parity-tests` features are empty placeholders.

Architectural ruling: complete the cutover with one branded composite Store/backend boundary and
one Runtime owner-fate coordinator. Keep the RFC/plan/manifest immutable. Replace the current
public low-level orchestration helpers rather than layering a compatibility facade; make the
application and transports consume the new owner-carrying lifecycle, and land the three preflight
artifacts plus the conformance/model harness before final verification. The detailed target API,
deletion scope, tests, and commit order are supplied by the architect audit in the continuation
record.

Continuation audit (2026-08-12): the prior completion claim was too strong. Current evidence
confirms the three-family journal, strict Program/Store validation, direct-new call gates, typed
Runtime owners, domain decoder hardening, PostgreSQL mechanical checks, and the final Nixfied gate,
but it does not yet prove every plan requirement. In particular, the checked-in U3/U5 inventory,
U8/U11 compile-spike/context table, and U1/U7/U9 measured capacity artifact are absent; Runtime
does not yet expose the plan's high-level admission/resume/drive owner-fate coordinator; Store has
no composite mechanical backend SPI or opened branded ports; PostgreSQL lacks executable crash/
multi-process conformance tests; and the requested counter/model/type-boundary matrices are only
partially represented by unit tests. These are active implementation items, not accepted limits.

The three preflight artifacts are now checked in:

- `docs/preflight/capability-binding-inventory.md` records the U3/U5 capability, factual-trust,
  absorption, and immutable descriptor inventory.
- `docs/preflight/cumulative-context-abi.md` records the two complete C0-to-result tables and
  the current U8/U11 typed-handoff evidence.
- `docs/preflight/capacity-envelope.md` records the fixed bounds, reservation law, fixtures, and
  the still-pending hot/cold high-water measurement harness.

2026-08-13 — continuation implementation and final audit

The architect ruling is implemented in commit `6400112a` (`complete single-trust runtime and
storage cutover`). The resulting tree now has one branded composite `StructuredStore::open`
boundary over the mechanical Memory/PostgreSQL SPI, consuming split history/configuration/audit
ports, cached qualified-run indexes, direct appended-prefix qualification, and configuration
owners that expose a revision only on direct success or an exact duplicate. PostgreSQL uses the
same SPI and the v8 three-family schema; its managed lane checks the admitted primary settings and
runs the shared frame/configuration CAS contract with two pooled connections.

Runtime now owns the exhaustive admission, resume, session, suspended-owner, and step outcomes.
`RunSession` retains the Store reduction and catalog-branded typed context. Hot conclusion handoff
uses the cached reduction and a suffix advance, while cold resume performs one bounded complete
prefix qualification and typed reification. The lifecycle regression records hot head 2 at 3,241
canonical frame bytes and cold head 3 at 4,550 bytes; its access regression counts provider entry,
accepted ingress, preparation, and interpretation separately and requires exactly one of each.
The finite owner-fate model proves that only one direct-new preparation can mint a call.

The application now uses live Runtime assemblies when supplied and rejects status-only success for
an actionable structural run. The maximum-entry-point fixture records the four-State EVM program at
4 declarations/6,840 canonical bytes and the 64-collection/64-source Portfolio plan at 963
declarations/1,566,954 canonical bytes. Runtime and Store API trybuild tests cover non-Clone
sessions and private Runtime/QualifiedRun construction; the shared Memory/PostgreSQL conformance
test covers idempotent found, stale-head, same-head frame races, configuration races, bounded raw
DTO constructors, facts, and audit rows.

Completed focused verification on this implementation:

- `nix develop -c cargo fmt --all`
- strict affected-package Clippy with `--all-targets --all-features -D warnings`
- affected Runtime, Store, App, and PostgreSQL checks/tests, including both trybuild suites
- `nix run .#run -- --task test-db` — managed PostgreSQL passed (`1 passed, 0 failed`)
- `nix run .#model-check`
- `nix run .#run -- --task negative-scan`
- `bash scripts/check-cutover-manifest.sh` and `git diff --check`

The RFC, implementation plan, and cutover manifest remain byte-for-byte unchanged. The remaining
documented trust-boundary limits are the primary crash/restart profile rather than host/failover
durability, trusted in-process Rust State/adapter code, process-local owner loss before durable
conclusion, and the two fixed entry points. The managed database task does not control the service
process to inject a primary crash; it proves the admitted settings, a fresh schema baseline, and
same-epoch CAS behavior, so no stronger crash-injection claim is recorded.

