2026-08-13 — corrective completion audit after independent gap review

Material uncertainties

none.

The preceding completion entry was written before the independent audit and is superseded for
acceptance purposes. That audit correctly identified six gaps: same-run conclusion classification,
fresh physical ids for repeated fact rebinding, preservation of permanent rejection owners,
producer provenance and Store-bound frontier validation, the PostgreSQL first-publication race,
and missing recovery-matrix evidence. The corrective implementation closes those gaps without
changing the normative inputs. Their SHA-256 identities remain:

- `RFC_REFACTOR_SINGLE_TRUST_BOUNDARY.md`: `d5293fe93e356748a364ca2832481548210a5b3b07f1fa5f0a92c4415735f46b`
- `IMPL_PLAN_RFC_REFACTOR_SINGLE_TRUST_BOUNDARY.md`: `185d5575fe30ff5041f76c540731259ce683c1069015f707821444ec12e72148`

The ordered corrective commits are:

- `b8eeefca` — enforce Store-owned fact and conclusion recovery boundaries;
- `c96c1f7f` — preserve conclusion classifications through Runtime recovery; and
- `a827449c` — serialize first fact publication across PostgreSQL backends.

Corrective evidence:

- Fact selections now carry aligned Store-authored source, producer Program, producer run/record,
  and producer-head provenance. Their frontier identity is bound to Store scope, writer epoch,
  and tenant. Cold qualification and preparation revalidate historical/future coordinates,
  publication heads, producer prefixes, exact producer heads, and exact fact values.
- Same-run conclusion races compare occurrence, mode/preparation, fact selection, evidence,
  outcome, and object closure while excluding sequence, recursive head, physical append id, and
  publication coordinate. Store returns `AlreadyConcludedSame`, `NoLongerSelected`, `Conflict`,
  or `InvalidHistory` in the required precedence and retains the owner on classifier/rebind
  failure. Access supersession, Pure same/conflict, repeated fresh fact-rebind ids, and unknown
  acknowledgement recovery have regression coverage; Runtime never re-enters State or adapters
  on these branches and exposes permanent rejection as an owner-bearing result.
- PostgreSQL materializes and locks a zero fact-head row before checking the first publication.
  Shared Memory/PostgreSQL conformance proves two independent first publishers produce exactly one
  commit and one `FactFrontierChanged`, with no partial loser run.
- Documentation records the Store/Runtime ownership, recovery matrix, frontier/provenance rules,
  fresh physical rebind identity, and PostgreSQL transaction invariant.

Verification after the corrective commits:

- focused Nix-shell tests: facts (3), Store all-features (19 plus API trybuild), Runtime (12 plus
  API trybuild), App (6), and the PostgreSQL crate check — all passed;
- `nix run .#model-check` — passed with model hash
  `e0a1835b7017bcfe0ec4aff72bacd78838a9973786b6810d5cd0befbca3f7251`;
- `nix run .#run -- --task negative-scan` — passed;
- `bash scripts/check-cutover-manifest.sh` — passed;
- `nix run .#ci` — `run-2543027-1786608382475382897`, all 7 tasks passed, including the managed
  PostgreSQL lane; and
- `git diff --check` and final immutable-input hash checks — passed.

The final correction commit records this audit entry. The deliberate durability and trust limits
